---
/**
 * Unix Timestamp Converter — 영국 영어판 다섯 번째 도구(유닉스 타임스탬프 변환). 한국어판 /tools/unix-time/ 와
 * 계산(lib/unix-time.ts)·스크립트(scripts/unix-time.ts 의 EN 사전 — 날짜는 <html lang="en-GB"> 로 'Wed, 21 Oct 2026')를
 * 같이 쓰고, 문장은 영국 영어로 새로 썼다(번역 아님). 조사표 영국 칸 '같음'(POSIX 4.19). 고친 공유 파일 없음.
 *
 * 소제목은 영국 구글 자동완성에서 왔다(keywords/gb-unix-time-2026-10-21.csv, hl en · gl gb):
 *   unix time · epoch time meaning / epoch time now · unix timestamp now / unix timestamp milliseconds /
 *   epoch converter timezone · unix timestamp to utc · epoch time to normal time / epoch converter excel /
 *   timestamp to date python · js · sql · postgresql · sql server · oracle / unix timestamp discord
 * 시간대는 UTC 와 '이 기기 시간대'만. 영국 숫자는 GOV.UK 에서 읽은 것만(확인 2026-10-21):
 *   https://www.gov.uk/when-do-the-clocks-change (Last updated 24 November 2025) — 3월 마지막 일요일 오전 1시에 1시간 앞으로,
 *   10월 마지막 일요일 오전 2시에 1시간 뒤로, 앞선 기간이 BST, 돌아가면 GMT. 2026-10-25 뒤로 · 2027-03-28 앞으로.
 * GMT·BST 의 UTC 오프셋 숫자는 GOV.UK 문장에 없어 본문에 숫자로 쓰지 않는다('1 hour ahead' 만).
 * 본문 예시 숫자는 전부 아래 frontmatter 에서 lib 함수로 계산한다.
 */
import BaseHead from "../../../../components/BaseHead.astro";
import AdSlot from "../../../../components/AdSlot.astro";
import ClarityHead from "../../../../components/ClarityHead.astro";
import GtagHead from "../../../../components/GtagHead.astro";
import TrackLinks from "../../../../components/TrackLinks.astro";
import AdSenseHead from "../../../../components/AdSenseHead.astro";
import SiteHeader from "../../../../components/SiteHeader.astro";
import SiteFooter from "../../../../components/SiteFooter.astro";
import "../../../../styles/global.css";
import { INT32_MAX, toParts, enText, isoText, secondsText, dateValue, timeValue } from "../../../../lib/unix-time";

const title = "Unix Timestamp Converter UK — Epoch Time to Date in UTC, GMT or BST";
const description =
  "Turn a 10-digit (seconds) or 13-digit (milliseconds) Unix timestamp into a UK-format date in UTC or your own time zone, and a date back to epoch time. Excel, SQL, Discord.";

const GB = "en-GB";
/** 처음 보이는 예 — 2026-10-21 09:30:00 UTC */
const BASE_MS = 1_792_575_000_000;
const p0 = toParts(BASE_MS, 0);
const BASE_S = secondsText(BASE_MS);
const zero = enText(toParts(0, 0), GB);
const minus1 = enText(toParts(-1000, 0), GB);
const y2038 = enText(toParts(INT32_MAX * 1000, 0), GB);
const wrap = enText(toParts(-(INT32_MAX + 1) * 1000, 0), GB);
const int32 = INT32_MAX.toLocaleString(GB);
---

<html lang="en-GB">
  <head>
    <BaseHead title={title} description={description} />
    <ClarityHead />
    <GtagHead />
    <AdSenseHead />
  </head>
  <body class="bg-texture">
    <SiteHeader />
    <main>
      <h1>Unix Timestamp Converter (Epoch Time ⇄ Date)</h1>
      <p>
        Got a long number such as {BASE_S} in a log file, a spreadsheet export or an API response? Paste it in and you'll see
        the date and time it represents, written the UK way (day, month, year). You can also go the other way: type a date and a
        time and the Unix timestamp appears. Whichever side you edit, the other one updates.
      </p>

      <div class="calc-card" data-two>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="zone">Time zone</label>
          <select id="zone" data-seg>
            <option value="utc" selected>UTC</option>
            <option value="device">Your time zone</option>
          </select>
          <p class="hint" id="zone-hint">The date and time fields and the result use this time zone. The timestamp itself never changes with it.</p>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="ts">Unix timestamp</label>
          <input type="text" id="ts" inputmode="decimal" autocomplete="off" spellcheck="false" value={BASE_S} placeholder="e.g. 1704067200" />
          <p class="hint" id="ts-hint">10 digits are read as seconds, 13 digits as milliseconds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="unit">Unit</label>
          <select id="unit" data-seg>
            <option value="auto" selected>Auto</option>
            <option value="s">Seconds</option>
            <option value="ms">Milliseconds</option>
          </select>
        </div>

        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="date">Date</label>
          <input type="date" id="date" value={dateValue(p0)} min="1900-01-01" max="9999-12-30" />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="time">Time (24-hour clock, hh:mm:ss)</label>
          <input type="time" id="time" step="1" value={timeValue(p0)} />
        </div>
        <div class="calc-input-wrap">
          <label for="msec">Milliseconds (0–999)</label>
          <input type="number" id="msec" inputmode="numeric" min="0" max="999" step="1" value="0" />
        </div>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-now" type="button" class="secondary">Now</button>
        </div>

        <div class="result-hero" aria-live="polite" aria-atomic="true" data-dock="hero-value">
          <p class="label" id="hero-label">UTC</p>
          <p class="big" id="hero-value">{enText(p0, GB)}</p>
          <p class="hint" id="hero-sub"></p>
        </div>

        <div class="stat-row" data-receipt id="receipt">
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Seconds (s)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-sec">{BASE_S}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">Milliseconds (ms)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-ms">{BASE_MS}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (UTC)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">ISO 8601 (chosen time zone)</span>
            <span class="value mono" id="v-iso-local">{isoText(BASE_MS, 0)}</span>
          </div>
          <div class="stat-chip">
            <span class="label">From now</span>
            <span class="value" id="v-rel">—</span>
          </div>
        </div>
        <p class="hint" id="note"></p>
        <p class="meta" id="now-line">Now: —</p>

        <div class="actions">
          <button id="btn-download" type="button">Save as image</button>
          <button id="btn-share" type="button" class="secondary">Share</button>
        </div>
      </div>

      <h2>Epoch time meaning: what a Unix timestamp counts</h2>
      <p>
        A Unix timestamp is a count of <strong>seconds since midnight UTC on 1 January 1970</strong>, a moment known as the
        epoch — hence "epoch time". The rule is set out in the POSIX standard (IEEE Std 1003.1): each day is treated as exactly
        86,400 seconds and leap seconds are left out. That makes 0 equal to {zero} UTC, and −1,000 milliseconds equal to
        {" "}{minus1} UTC.
      </p>

      <h2>Epoch time now</h2>
      <p>
        The "Now" line beneath the converter gives the current Unix time in seconds and milliseconds, updated every second. Tap
        <strong>Now</strong> to load the current timestamp into the converter. Both readings come from your own device's clock,
        so they're only as right as that clock is.
      </p>

      <h2>10 digits or 13? Seconds versus milliseconds</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>10 digits (e.g. {BASE_S})</strong> — seconds. Any moment between September 2001 and November 2286 is a 10-digit
          number.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>13 digits (e.g. {BASE_MS})</strong> — milliseconds, the form returned by JavaScript's <code>Date.now()</code> and
          Java's <code>System.currentTimeMillis()</code>. Divide by 1,000 to get seconds.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>How Auto works</strong> — a number with a decimal point counts as seconds; a whole number of up to 11 digits is
          seconds, and 12 digits or more is milliseconds. Commas and spaces are stripped out. If the guess is wrong, choose the
          unit yourself.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>UTC, GMT and BST: epoch time to normal UK time</h2>
      <p>
        A timestamp doesn't belong to any time zone — it pins down one instant for the whole world. The converter shows that
        instant in <strong>UTC</strong> and, underneath, in whatever time zone your device is set to. Pick
        {" "}<strong>Your time zone</strong> if you'd rather read and type dates in local time.
      </p>
      <p>
        On a device set to UK time, that local time moves twice a year. According to GOV.UK, the clocks go forward 1 hour at 1am
        on the last Sunday in March and back 1 hour at 2am on the last Sunday in October; the period when they're 1 hour ahead
        is British Summer Time (BST), and the rest of the year is Greenwich Mean Time (GMT). The next changes are
        {" "}<strong>25 October 2026</strong> (back) and <strong>28 March 2027</strong> (forward). So two timestamps from July and
        December can show different offsets in "Your time zone" even though nothing is wrong — the ISO 8601 line spells out the
        offset used for each one.
      </p>

      <h2>Epoch converter in Excel or Google Sheets</h2>
      <p>
        A spreadsheet keeps dates as serial numbers in which one day is 1. Divide the seconds by 86,400 and add the epoch date.
        With a timestamp in seconds in cell A1:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Date and time in UTC: <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>13-digit milliseconds: <code>=A1/86400000+DATE(1970,1,1)</code></li>
        <li>Local time: add your offset from UTC in hours divided by 24, <code>=A1/86400+DATE(1970,1,1)+(offset/24)</code>. In the UK the offset is 1 hour bigger during BST than during GMT, so one fixed number won't suit a whole year of data</li>
        <li>UTC date and time back to a timestamp: <code>=(A1-DATE(1970,1,1))*86400</code></li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you get a bare number, format the cell as a date and time — <code>dd/mm/yyyy hh:mm:ss</code> gives the UK order.
      </p>

      <h2>Timestamp to date in Python, JavaScript and SQL</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Python</strong>: <code>datetime.fromtimestamp({BASE_S}, tz=timezone.utc)</code> (after <code>from datetime import datetime, timezone</code>)</li>
        <li><strong>pandas</strong>: <code>pd.to_datetime({BASE_S}, unit="s")</code> — <code>unit="ms"</code> for milliseconds</li>
        <li><strong>JavaScript</strong>: <code>new Date({BASE_S} * 1000)</code> — seconds must be multiplied by 1,000 first</li>
        <li><strong>PostgreSQL</strong>: <code>SELECT to_timestamp({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>MySQL</strong>: <code>SELECT FROM_UNIXTIME({BASE_S});</code></li>
        <li><strong>SQL Server</strong>: <code>SELECT DATEADD(second, {BASE_S}, '1970-01-01');</code></li>
        <li><strong>Oracle</strong>: <code>SELECT DATE '1970-01-01' + {BASE_S}/86400 FROM dual;</code> — adding a number to a DATE adds days</li>
        <li><strong>Linux / macOS shell</strong>: <code>date +%s</code> prints the current timestamp; GNU <code>date -d @{BASE_S}</code> converts one</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Not sure a result is right? Paste the same number into the converter above and compare.</p>

      <h2>Unix timestamps in Discord</h2>
      <p>
        Type <code>&lt;t:{BASE_S}:F&gt;</code> in a Discord message and each reader sees the date in their own time zone — handy
        for a server with members in the UK and abroad. The letter after the second colon picks the style: <code>t</code> and
        {" "}<code>T</code> for short and long time, <code>d</code> and <code>D</code> for short and long date, <code>f</code> and
        {" "}<code>F</code> for date and time, and <code>R</code> for relative time ("in 3 days"). Use the seconds value, not
        milliseconds.
      </p>

      <h2>The 2038 problem</h2>
      <p>
        The largest signed 32-bit integer is {int32}. Read as a Unix timestamp, that's {y2038} UTC. One second later, any system
        still storing time as a signed 32-bit number overflows and jumps to a negative value — {wrap} UTC. The converter adds a
        warning below the result for any time beyond that point.
      </p>

      <details>
        <summary>What about dates before 1970?</summary>
        <p>
          They're negative: −1 is one second before the epoch, {minus1} UTC. The POSIX formula is written
          for 1970 onwards, but like most programming languages this converter carries on counting backwards at 86,400 seconds a
          day. It covers 1 January 1900 up to the year 9999.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Can I paste a timestamp with a decimal point?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes — it's read as seconds with up to three decimal places (milliseconds). Microsecond and nanosecond timestamps (16
          and 19 digits) aren't supported.
        </p>
      </details>

      <details>
        <summary>Why is my result an hour out?</summary>
        <p>
          In the UK that's usually BST: the number was read as UTC while you expected local summer time, or the other way round.
          Switch between <strong>UTC</strong> and <strong>Your time zone</strong> and compare. If the date lands in 1970 or far in
          the future, the unit is wrong by a factor of 1,000 — choose seconds or milliseconds by hand.
        </p>
      </details>

      <p>
        To count the days between two dates, use the <a href="/en-gb/tools/date-diff/">date calculator</a>; for a countdown to a
        particular day, the <a href="/en-gb/tools/dday/">days until countdown</a>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sources: <a href="https://pubs.opengroup.org/onlinepubs/9799919799/basedefs/V1_chap04.html" rel="noopener">The Open Group Base Specifications Issue 8 (IEEE Std 1003.1-2024), 4.19 Seconds Since the Epoch</a>
        {" "}· <a href="https://www.gov.uk/when-do-the-clocks-change" rel="noopener">GOV.UK — When do the clocks change?</a> (both checked 21 October 2026)
      </p>

      <AdSlot label="unix-time-result-en-gb" />
    </main>
    <SiteFooter />
    <style>
      .mono { font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums; overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
      #hero-value { overflow-wrap: anywhere; }
    </style>
    <script src="../../../../scripts/unix-time.ts"></script>
    <TrackLinks />
    <script src="../../../../scripts/calc-ui.ts"></script>
  </body>
</html>
